<section class="section" id="sec-overview">
  <h2>How Hospitals Make &amp; Spend Money</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">A hospital is simultaneously a clinical institution, a business enterprise, and a community anchor. Understanding its economics requires grasping how revenue flows in, where expenses accumulate, and what metrics determine viability.</p>

  <h3>The Revenue Waterfall</h3>
  <p>Hospital revenue starts with <strong>gross patient charges</strong> — the full "sticker price" of all services delivered. But no one actually pays this amount. Through a series of deductions, the gross revenue is reduced to what the hospital actually collects:</p>

  <div class="flow-row">
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--accent);">Gross Charges<br><small>$50M</small></div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--orange);">− Contractual<br>Adjustments<br><small>($20M)</small></div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--orange);">− Charity Care<br><small>($2M)</small></div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--orange);">− Bad Debt<br><small>($1.5M)</small></div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--green);">Net Patient<br>Revenue<br><small>$26.5M</small></div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key Insight:</strong> On average, hospitals collect only about 35–55 cents for every dollar they bill. The difference between gross charges and net revenue — driven by contractual adjustments negotiated with insurers — is the single largest financial dynamic in hospital economics.
  </div>

  <h3>Where the Money Goes</h3>
  <div class="chart-box">
    <div class="chart-title">Hospital Expense Distribution — National Average</div>
    <canvas id="chartExpenses" height="260"></canvas>
  </div>

  <details>
    <summary>Understanding Each Expense Category</summary>
    <div class="detail-body">
      <p><strong>Salaries &amp; Benefits (55–60%):</strong> The largest expense. Includes physicians, nurses, allied health, administrative staff, and all associated benefits (health insurance, retirement, payroll taxes). For rural hospitals, the travel nurse premium can inflate this category significantly.</p>
      <p><strong>Supplies &amp; Drugs (15–18%):</strong> Medical supplies, pharmaceuticals, implants, surgical supplies. Highly variable based on case mix and service lines. Drug costs have been rising faster than general inflation.</p>
      <p><strong>Purchased Services (8–10%):</strong> Contracted services like IT, billing, laundry, food service, locum tenens, and consulting. Rural hospitals often outsource more due to inability to maintain specialized staff.</p>
      <p><strong>Depreciation &amp; Interest (5–8%):</strong> The cost of using capital assets (buildings, equipment) over time, plus the interest on debt used to finance them. Old facilities have low depreciation but high maintenance costs.</p>
      <p><strong>Other Operating (8–12%):</strong> Utilities, insurance (malpractice, property), maintenance, marketing, education, and miscellaneous operational costs.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

