<section class="section" id="sec-revenue">
  <h2>The Revenue Cycle</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">The revenue cycle encompasses every financial step from patient registration through final payment collection. It typically spans 30–90+ days and involves dozens of handoffs between clinical, coding, billing, and collections departments.</p>

  <div class="flow-row" style="flex-wrap: wrap;">
    <div class="flow-box" style="background:var(--blue-bg); border-color:var(--blue);">1. Scheduling<br>&amp; Registration</div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="background:var(--blue-bg); border-color:var(--blue);">2. Insurance<br>Verification</div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="background:var(--green-bg); border-color:var(--green);">3. Clinical<br>Service Delivery</div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="background:var(--green-bg); border-color:var(--green);">4. Charge<br>Capture</div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="background:var(--purple-bg); border-color:var(--purple);">5. Medical<br>Coding</div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="background:var(--purple-bg); border-color:var(--purple);">6. Claim<br>Submission</div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="background:var(--orange-bg); border-color:var(--orange);">7. Payment<br>Posting</div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="background:var(--orange-bg); border-color:var(--orange);">8. Denial<br>Management</div>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="flow-box" style="background:var(--gold-bg); border-color:var(--gold);">9. Patient<br>Collections</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Revenue Leakage Points</h3>
  <p>Revenue leakage occurs at every stage. The most common and costly leakage points are:</p>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Leakage Point</th><th>Typical Impact</th><th>Root Cause</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Missed Charges</td><td class="num">2–5% of revenue</td><td>Services performed but not documented in charge system</td><td>Automated charge reconciliation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Coding Errors</td><td class="num">1–3% of revenue</td><td>Undercoding, upcoding (compliance risk), missed modifiers</td><td>CDI programs, coder education</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Claim Denials</td><td class="num">5–10% of claims</td><td>Authorization failures, medical necessity, timely filing</td><td>Prior auth automation, root cause analysis</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Underpayments</td><td class="num">1–2% of revenue</td><td>Payer remittance below contracted rate</td><td>Contract management software</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bad Debt</td><td class="num">3–8% of revenue</td><td>Patient inability or unwillingness to pay</td><td>Financial counseling, payment plans</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>For Critical Access Hospitals:</strong> Revenue leakage has a unique double impact. Because Medicare reimburses at 101% of <em>cost</em>, missed charges that reduce revenue also reduce the costs reported on the cost report — lowering future Medicare reimbursement. Every dollar of leakage can cost the CAH $2.01 in lost reimbursement.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 3: REIMBURSEMENT ═══════════════ -->

<section class="section" id="sec-reimbursement">
  <h2>How Hospitals Get Paid</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">No single payment model applies to all hospitals. The reimbursement a hospital receives depends on the payer type, the hospital's Medicare designation, and the negotiated contract terms.</p>

  <div class="tab-bar" id="reimTabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="showTab('reimTabs','reimContent','tab-medicare')">Medicare</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showTab('reimTabs','reimContent','tab-medicaid')">Medicaid</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showTab('reimTabs','reimContent','tab-commercial')">Commercial</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="showTab('reimTabs','reimContent','tab-selfpay')">Self-Pay</button>
  </div>
  <div id="reimContent">
    <div class="tab-content active" id="tab-medicare">
      <h4>Medicare Reimbursement Models</h4>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-header"><div class="icon" style="background:var(--blue-bg);color:var(--blue);">⚕</div> Prospective Payment (PPS)</div>
          <p>Most acute care hospitals receive <strong>fixed payments</strong> based on <span class="tip" data-tip="Diagnosis-Related Groups classify patients into ~750 categories based on diagnosis, procedures, age, sex, and complications.">DRG classification</span>. The payment amount is predetermined — regardless of the hospital's actual costs.</p>
          <div class="formula-box">
            <span class="sym">Payment</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="sym">DRG_Weight</span> <span class="op">×</span> <span class="sym">Base_Rate</span> <span class="op">×</span> <span class="sym">Wage_Index</span><br>
            <span class="comment">// Adjusted for teaching, DSH, outlier cases</span>
          </div>
          <p><span class="badge badge-blue">Key Point</span> Under PPS, the hospital profits if costs are below the DRG payment, and loses money if costs exceed it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-header"><div class="icon" style="background:var(--green-bg);color:var(--green);">🏥</div> Cost-Based (CAH)</div>
          <p>Critical Access Hospitals receive <strong>101% of reasonable costs</strong> as determined by the annual Medicare Cost Report (CMS Form 2552-10). This is fundamentally different from PPS — the hospital is reimbursed for what it spent, not a fixed amount.</p>
          <div class="formula-box">
            <span class="sym">Reimbursement</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="sym">Allowable_Costs</span> <span class="op">×</span> <span class="sym">Medicare_Share</span> <span class="op">×</span> <span class="sym">1.01</span><br>
            <span class="comment">// Determined by step-down cost allocation methodology</span>
          </div>
          <p><span class="badge badge-green">Key Point</span> CAH reimbursement incentivizes accurate cost reporting rather than volume maximization.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px;">
        <div class="card-header"><div class="icon" style="background:var(--purple-bg);color:var(--purple);">🚑</div> Rural Emergency Hospital (REH)</div>
        <p>REHs receive <strong>OPPS rates + 5%</strong> for outpatient services, plus a <strong>monthly facility payment</strong> of $272,866 (FY2026). No inpatient services are provided. This model was created in 2023 for struggling small rural hospitals and former CAHs. 43 hospitals have converted as of early 2026.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" id="tab-medicaid">
      <h4>Medicaid Reimbursement</h4>
      <p>Medicaid reimbursement varies dramatically by state and is <strong>almost universally below cost</strong>. The average Medicaid operating margin for hospitals is approximately <strong>-18%</strong>, meaning hospitals lose about 18 cents on every dollar of Medicaid cost. Medicaid rate-setting is controlled at the state level, with some states using fee-for-service models and others using managed care organizations.</p>
      <p>For rural hospitals, Medicaid's share of revenue depends heavily on whether the state has expanded Medicaid under the ACA. In expansion states, more low-income adults are covered — reducing bad debt but at below-cost reimbursement rates. In non-expansion states, more patients are uninsured, increasing uncompensated care.</p>
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top:16px;">
        <div class="metric warn"><span class="metric-val">-18%</span><span class="metric-label">Avg. Medicaid Margin</span></div>
        <div class="metric neutral"><span class="metric-val">~20%</span><span class="metric-label">Share of Rural Volume</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" id="tab-commercial">
      <h4>Commercial Insurance</h4>
      <p>Commercial payers (employer-sponsored insurance, ACA marketplace plans) typically reimburse at rates <strong>above cost</strong>, making them the primary source of positive margin for most hospitals. Reimbursement rates are set through negotiations between the hospital and each insurer, with rates varying widely based on the hospital's negotiating leverage.</p>
      <p>System-affiliated hospitals generally achieve higher commercial rates than independent hospitals due to greater negotiating leverage. Research shows commercial margins of <strong>16.4%</strong> for independent CAHs versus <strong>25.2%</strong> for system-affiliated CAHs.</p>
      <div class="grid grid-3" style="margin-top:16px;">
        <div class="metric good"><span class="metric-val">+25%</span><span class="metric-label">System-affiliated CAH Margin</span></div>
        <div class="metric good"><span class="metric-val">+16%</span><span class="metric-label">Independent CAH Margin</span></div>
        <div class="metric neutral"><span class="metric-val">~15%</span><span class="metric-label">Share of Rural Volume</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tab-content" id="tab-selfpay">
      <h4>Self-Pay &amp; Uninsured</h4>
      <p>Self-pay patients include both uninsured individuals and insured patients with high-deductible plans who owe significant out-of-pocket amounts. For rural hospitals, self-pay often represents 5–10% of volume but a disproportionate share of bad debt. Collecting from self-pay patients is challenging: rural communities often have lower median incomes, and aggressive collection practices can damage the hospital's community reputation.</p>
      <p>Charity care policies allow hospitals to forgive bills for patients below certain income thresholds, converting potential bad debt into a documented community benefit. Under cost-based reimbursement, Medicare reimburses only 65% of bad debt expenses for CAHs (reduced from 100% in previous years — a $159M annual impact across rural hospitals).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Payer Mix: The Most Critical Variable</h3>
